WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.850
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.340
- Welcome to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.210
Today, I'm honored and
privileged to host Sean Astin

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:12.940
for a conversation about mental health,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.290
resources advocacy and support.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.100
Sean's someone who's probably
known to all if not or many,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.510
if not all of you but I'm gonna go ahead

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.490
and give my really brief introduction.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:23.890
So Sean Astin is an American film actor,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.520
director, voice artists, producer,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.860
who's best known for his roles

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.667
in films like "The Lord
Of the Rings Trilogy",

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:31.800
"The Goonies" of course, "Rudy"

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.220
and also in a Netflix six
series, "Stranger Things Two".

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.140
He's a son of Academy Award
winning actress, Patty Duke,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.370
and acclaimed actor, John Astin.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.047
He's also won several different awards.

00:00:42.047 --> 00:00:44.400
"The Lord of the Rings
Trilogy" garnered eight

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.870
or 11, excuse me, 11 Academy Awards.

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:50.330
And Sean also has taken home awards for,

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.880
he had the standard award
for best supporting actor,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.010
awards from the Las Vegas
Film Critics Society.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.540
the Seattle Film Critics,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.230
the Utah Film Critics Association,

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.460
and the Phoenix Film Critics Society.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.332
So obviously someone who's talented

00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:05.470
in his field and in his vocation.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.630
He's also a long distance runner.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.540
has been a long distance
runner since his teens,

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:11.780
and in 2012, Astin launched
a Twitter campaign,

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:15.000
hashtag Run3rd as a way
to dedicate his runs

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.410
to causes and ideas that
matter not only to him

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.430
but to others as well.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.020
He's a UCLA graduate who
has served on the board

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.760
of several different national
nonprofit organizations,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.330
including the National
Center For Family Literacy.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.240
He's a vocal advocate for literacy,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.830
mental health awareness, bipolar disorder,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.990
civic engagement and several other issues.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.940
So I'm honored to welcome, Sean Astin

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.370
to the Addy Hour podcast.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.258
Thanks so much for being here.

00:01:39.258 --> 00:01:40.973
- Its so nice to hear you read all that.

00:01:40.973 --> 00:01:43.880
I just made it feel so I don't
know, substantial somehow.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.480
That's nice. Thank you for that.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:46.420
- It is substantial.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.807
Substantial and real.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:48.990
(both laughing)

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.980
- It's real.
- It's real.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.030
But definitely appreciate, you know,

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.680
all the work that you've
done over the years

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.770
in so many different spheres,

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.140
not just in terms of
obviously your talent,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.470
onscreen and offscreen but also just

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.880
the way you've been a real
advocate for mental health.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.880
And I know there's a story there

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.660
that we'll get into as well.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:06.493
But as my listeners know,

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:08.180
I often just like to
check in and kind of see

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.380
how my guests are doing
in the midst of everything

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.750
that we're facing in this
ever-changing world and emerging,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:14.990
trying to emerge from COVID.

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:15.980
So I just wanted to start there

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.490
and see how you're doing
these days overall.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:19.730
- Well, thank you for that.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:21.700
As people are watching this,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.350
I'm currently sitting in a little

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.730
cottage in Sweden

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.466
and there at any moment I
fully expect one of these big,

00:02:32.466 --> 00:02:33.600
you know like cruise ships that go by.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.300
They're like dinner cruise,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.430
but they're the size of big cruise ships.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.940
I am back to life,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.550
I would say, you know,

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.530
I went and made a movie in Louisiana

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.330
and in the film industry

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.033
there's all sorts of COVID
protocols that films take.

00:02:51.734 --> 00:02:52.730
But it was weird.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.370
It was definitely weird to do
that first sort of, you know,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.090
I was at the airport with crowds

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.120
and in the airport shuttle
to the rental car with,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.410
you know, crowded buses
and just thinking what,

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.880
you know, what has happened
to my consciousness?

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.760
This is not just fear of

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.670
you know, of getting COVID,

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.540
it's also my relationship,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:18.240
all of our relationship to each other

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.150
and to the outside world.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:22.310
And so that was a culture
shock after a year and a half.

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:24.827
And then, then when I came
back, my wife and kids are like,

00:03:24.827 --> 00:03:27.300
"You can't come in yet,
you have to stay next door.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.260
You don't have to get like
an Airbnb for a few days,

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.420
so you can be tested."

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:34.210
And so I did that and now
I'm over here doing these

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.583
conventions that are like autograph,

00:03:38.088 --> 00:03:41.110
you know, science fiction,
fantasy comic book, you know,

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:42.790
Comicon kind of conventions.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.920
So there it's a lot of people,

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.730
so it's gonna be and I did it in Sweden

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:49.060
and then I'm going to do Wales.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:49.893
So I don't know,

00:03:49.893 --> 00:03:51.790
I feel like the treadmill
has started back up.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:53.570
I'm back on it.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:55.370
You know, I'm getting this a

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.170
Master's in Public
Administration, Public Policy

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:03.050
from American University
and I am spending all of my

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:07.010
discretionary time on, you know,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.110
reading and studying and quizzes
and midterms and all that.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.610
So life is good.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:12.443
Life is good.

00:04:12.443 --> 00:04:14.840
It's the best is that I'm
able to like get outside

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.140
and do long walks and stuff.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.670
So, but it's hard.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.840
The hardest part is family.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.010
Is managing family.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:27.050
But everybody seems to be in a mode.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.520
You know, the middle kid is

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:31.353
so I've got three daughters.

00:04:31.353 --> 00:04:32.620
Allie, Elizabeth and Isabella,

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.160
Allie lives with her boyfriend

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.260
and they have their own place now.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.390
They were with us for a
long time during COVID

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:39.200
and then they got their own place.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.110
And then Elizabeth is still at home.

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.520
She's at Pepperdine and
she's still at home.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.290
So wait, I'm figuring any minute,

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:48.470
that'll be both she and the youngest one

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:49.720
who's 16 are at home so

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.140
it's gonna be amazing to see what happens

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.340
when we're no longer living
in a submarine environment.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.120
You know, where you just,

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:58.350
where you're outside of our cohort.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.590
And who knows what that holds in store.

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:03.420
But life is good, long
answer to a short question.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:04.630
- I appreciate that honesty.

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.323
And just taking us
through that journey too.

00:05:06.323 --> 00:05:08.400
I mean, it sounds like
some of it's pacing too,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.120
you know, as you know,
the families kind of all

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.950
been together I'm assuming
more often than before

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:13.947
from the way that you've characterized it.

00:05:13.947 --> 00:05:16.130
But even as you are emerging kind of...

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.830
- I actually, for a long time,

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:21.000
I would mourn the end of COVID

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.320
because it would likely
mean that we'd never spend

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.140
that kind of close time
together ever again.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:30.330
So in that sense, as a father who

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.360
and a guy who traveled around

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.460
all, you know, so many
weeks out of the year,

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:39.300
the idea that I kind of liked it.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.730
You know, I kinda liked it,

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:43.670
but you can tell that the long-term impact

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:45.010
is not ultimately healthy.

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.730
We, you know, they have to be with friends

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:48.830
and they have to be,

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:50.030
we all have to be with friends.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:53.520
We all have to move our bodies around and,

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.200
but it's, you know, we know
our personalities now so well,

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.187
you know, you can almost do like

00:06:00.187 --> 00:06:02.660
hand gestures, a little facial, you know.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.530
Like, oh, that one just
came down and, you know,

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.030
you can tell that, you know,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.224
they're in a mood, leave him alone,

00:06:07.224 --> 00:06:08.428
you know, kind of a thing.

00:06:08.428 --> 00:06:09.261
- Yeah.

00:06:09.261 --> 00:06:10.416
Did that catch you off guard?

00:06:10.416 --> 00:06:11.514
Do you think you have a,

00:06:11.514 --> 00:06:12.970
like you and your family
have more of an appreciation

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.150
for that now or was that

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.610
something that you think
was always there and

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.443
just kind of continued?

00:06:18.443 --> 00:06:21.260
- Well, they all had their ecosystem

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:25.140
and I would come in and
out of the ecosystem

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.090
based on my travels and
all the work I was doing.

00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:32.210
So I definitely felt

00:06:35.611 --> 00:06:39.160
their little cocoon

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.300
expanded to include me during it.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.120
But then in a weird way, you know,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.210
I started doing from
home work with my union.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.650
You know, zoom kinda made
lots of things possible

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:51.690
that weren't possible beforehand

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:55.170
so I applied to American University.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.120
I started doing this thing.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.020
And a weird way we kind
of like went to our

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.100
mental neutral corners anyway.
- Yeah.

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.290
- Even though we were, I
wanted to learn the guitar.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.183
So I'd be upstairs, you know,

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:06.830
with the door closed playing guitar.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.940
And it's amazing after 10,000 hours

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.963
of guitar blank how bad I still am.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.350
And they're very patient, you know,

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:14.497
they'll come up and go
to bed and I'm like,

00:07:14.497 --> 00:07:15.890
"Wait, you got to listen to this song."

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:16.723
You know, they're like, "Yeah,

00:07:16.723 --> 00:07:18.873
I'm not gonna listen
to it, it's all right."

00:07:18.873 --> 00:07:21.680
But you know, we

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:24.880
for a while when COVID started we all ate

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.400
sort of like regular meals together,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.290
like normal people.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.070
But then we kind of all live
like we're in a fish tank

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:34.310
or, you know, like we all just kind of

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.203
graze and do what we're doing.

00:07:37.514 --> 00:07:38.883
So it's amazing how we've,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.950
settled back into like
where we were beforehand

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.261
in a lot of ways.

00:07:45.261 --> 00:07:46.190
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.620
Well, it sounds like you
all were flexible too.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.770
I mean, you kind of
readjusted in this mode...

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.130
- Well, we were lucky.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:52.670
We were lucky in,

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:56.500
I will forever be grateful for
our particular circumstance

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:59.150
in this sort of thing.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.610
You can see the rhythms

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.280
of your day and your week and your month.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.640
You can see your family rhythms.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:09.520
You can see your own
psychological rhythms.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.100
You know, why on one day

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.340
am I feeling totally claustrophobic?

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:15.950
And like, I have to get out
and there's no reason to,

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:17.430
but and other days,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.620
no there's, you know,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.440
we want to go for a
drive just to get a sense

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.100
of what's going on in the outside world.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.940
So like we're in the Mars Rover

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.240
and we're going out to explore.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:26.823
You know, and like what,

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:29.763
how do we,

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:34.040
you know, no one got hurt physically.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:35.930
I wondered about that a lot.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:38.330
Like, what happens if
somebody falls down the stairs

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:42.293
or cuts themselves or
has some other, you know,

00:08:43.210 --> 00:08:44.540
breaks a tooth or I don't know.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:47.930
Something like it's been a
blessing that nobody's been

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.530
really injured over
the course of the time.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.140
But because of that,

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:57.140
we've basically been in this
little behavioral experiment.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:00.103
And, you know, I,

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:04.300
yeah, I mean, I think
we'll be unpacking it

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.620
for the rest of our lives.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:09.150
I as our family member, I've
been the sort of first to go.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:13.010
And so and they, you know,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:14.360
we have this long talk about like

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:15.700
you're gonna go into in this movie

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.210
and then you're gonna come back
and you're going to be like,

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.710
there's no such thing as
COVID, let's just keep going.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.150
I'm like, probably because
it was in the south

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:22.150
where I was working,

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:25.720
but I said, no, you know,
of course I'll be respectful

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.300
and it's all I'm masked it all doing

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.440
what we're supposed to do.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.760
But when I came back, I
started looking at them

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:33.298
and judging them a little bit.

00:09:33.298 --> 00:09:35.180
Like you guys, you can't,

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.613
your paradigm is.

00:09:37.613 --> 00:09:39.690
You're in a locked paradigm
that's not in your own interest.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.400
You've got to break out of it.

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.250
And then there was like
this civil war in there.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:49.110
This cold civil war in our house about,

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:53.440
you know, whether what's
it's time to do and not do.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.690
But, you know, I'm taking
this economics class

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:59.820
and I'm like, come up with,

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:00.990
you know, things in everyday life

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.410
that reflect some of the economics,

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.600
you know, principles that we're studying.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.133
And one of the things
is like my whole life,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.100
I've as a parent,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.450
it's been about saving
money for their college.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.040
That's been my life.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.110
So save money for college.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:19.170
Save money for college.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.570
And we've been lucky to do it.

00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:22.530
Some years we couldn't,
other years they could.

00:10:22.530 --> 00:10:23.670
But by the time, the first one,

00:10:23.670 --> 00:10:26.650
by the time Allie got
ready to go to college

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:28.510
we've been able to kind of be there.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:32.390
But now that Elizabeth is in
her second year at Pepperdine,

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:33.933
her whole first year virtual.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.810
And you're looking at this bucket of money

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.860
that you worked so hard to save
over two decades basically,

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.330
and you go, boy, I've never
thought of this before.

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:50.860
We are like, well, I can just
park that money in a house.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:52.193
I just buy her a house.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.020
And somehow and,

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.430
so you start looking at like,

00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:01.143
what are she joined a sorority?

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:03.770
Its like she,

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:06.450
she knew that it wasn't okay

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.810
that we're so segregated from people.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:12.480
So she did it as a way
to make sure that she had

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:14.610
this virtual community so
that when we come out of it

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:15.740
she'll be able to go back into it.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.380
Which just is very sensible.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:20.620
But it seemed to me that

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:22.910
that activity,

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:25.480
that discretionary activity

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:30.280
that is so outside of her normal mode

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:34.180
is the thing that's worth
not buying the house for.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:38.380
I want her to go to college,
but my worldview has

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:43.380
you know, just paused to allow
other things to come into it.

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:44.470
What do we value?

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:45.470
Why do we value?

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:50.357
What does the opportunity
cost of doing that forego?

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.530
You know, so anyhow,

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:54.849
they're very patient with me.

00:11:54.849 --> 00:11:56.750
(all laughing)

00:11:56.750 --> 00:11:59.290
But yeah and I hope everybody is

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:01.160
going through those kinds of

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.590
evaluations in their mind.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.619
I think it wakes people up.

00:12:05.619 --> 00:12:06.452
- Yeah.

00:12:06.452 --> 00:12:07.990
Yeah, I think just having that awareness

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:08.823
like you mentioned too,

00:12:08.823 --> 00:12:11.190
of all the different values that we have.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:12.410
And I think it's given us another way

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:14.030
to actually look at those in the mirror

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.570
and think about what our values

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.610
and what our worldview are, you know.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:19.450
Cause even as you mentioned,

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:20.903
when you left and came back,

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:22.930
it seemed like there was a shift there

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.380
there's a little bit of a riffle.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:26.250
I mean, obviously we could get into that,

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:28.690
just all the different covert perspectives

00:12:28.690 --> 00:12:31.060
and different pockets of
the country but then just,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:32.940
you know, even that you've
caught yourself in that like

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.130
judging mode...

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:36.176
- We the film that I did

00:12:36.176 --> 00:12:39.100
the crew all has to be double vaxed.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.020
The crew has to all wear masks.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:43.600
Anybody that's interacting with actors

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.430
because actors have makeup
on that the masks can

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:48.330
smudge and mess up.

00:12:48.330 --> 00:12:53.330
So they have to have the
masks and the shield.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.050
You know, there's not,
there's all, you know,

00:12:56.050 --> 00:12:57.910
people are having temperature
checks in the morning.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:01.660
There was all these like
super rigorous controls.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:03.610
But the second I stepped outside

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:04.960
and I didn't have a mask on,

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:06.639
it was like no time.

00:13:06.639 --> 00:13:07.963
(all laughing)

00:13:07.963 --> 00:13:08.945
That was fun.

00:13:08.945 --> 00:13:10.001
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:10.001 --> 00:13:11.250
That's exactly what happens.

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:14.000
So I mean, it's that tension
too between because I mean,

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:15.610
so the other thing is you were there.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:17.450
I assume you didn't get sick.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:20.710
But a different scenario,
if something had happened,

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:21.550
you caught COVID

00:13:21.550 --> 00:13:23.370
and then how that could have
also changed your worldview.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:24.730
And I think some of that

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:27.370
has also played out in a lot of ways too.

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:29.630
- My brother and I we're talking because,

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:32.650
I'm in Sweden now and no
one in Sweden wears masks,

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:33.620
No one.

00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:36.500
So I'm interacting with say
a thousand people, right?

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:38.350
Or directly rely,

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:39.370
you know, I mean you're in a hall

00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:40.750
where there's 10,000 people

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:43.440
but directly interacting with
a couple of hundred maybe.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.963
And he's interacted with like six people,

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.620
but he's like, if I get COVID,

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.710
Sean gets COVID because he
was interacting with people,

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:56.000
it's justifiable because
he was making money.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.880
If I get COVID because I
was visiting my friends,

00:13:58.880 --> 00:13:59.713
then it was just,

00:13:59.713 --> 00:14:02.980
it was a discretionary
like error or something.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:07.083
So there's these kinds of psychological,

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:12.530
like games that you play
with yourself that go into,

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:18.620
you know, and so in
economics there's this thing,

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:20.453
they call it homo economicus.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.903
And that's the ideal rational actor.

00:14:26.185 --> 00:14:28.460
The entity that will
make whatever interests,

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.980
not gonna tip at a restaurant because

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:33.940
it's not in that person's interest

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:35.350
unless you have a different relationship.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.513
So you just start realizing
like what, you know,

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:42.820
what is our obligation to each other?

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:43.653
- Yeah.

00:14:43.653 --> 00:14:45.386
- What is our obligation to each other to,

00:14:45.386 --> 00:14:49.790
and how we, you know, the
only kind of leadership,

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:51.130
the best kind of leadership to me

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:53.120
is the only kind of
leadership of that by example.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:57.163
And what happens when you set
an example that, you know,

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.000
doesn't line up with the stated...

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:03.163
- Yeah.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:05.380
There's so many layers of that too,

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:08.360
because like your own
perspective about justifying

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:09.500
why you're doing what you're doing

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:10.910
versus what other people are thinking

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:12.110
versus on what the outcomes.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:14.340
'Cause like you and your
brother kind of depended on,

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:15.320
you know, if you got COVID,

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.830
if he had gotten COVID
different perspectives

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:18.160
on the outcomes that justification,

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:20.390
like all that kind of all mixed together.

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:21.330
I think that can,

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:22.294
it's good for us...

00:15:22.294 --> 00:15:25.033
- Then I have all of these friends,

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.467
you know, there's different
threads that I'm on

00:15:29.467 --> 00:15:31.040
and on one of these threads that I'm on

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.570
with a lot of very intelligent,

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:35.250
very, you know, well-educated people,

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:39.240
there's a steady drum beat of

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:44.070
antagonism towards public
policy with regard to COVID

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:47.900
and the memes and the videos and the

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.193
things that come through.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:51.580
They're all smart people.

00:15:51.580 --> 00:15:53.340
These people I'm describing
who are putting it forward.

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:57.480
So there's always like some
element in what's being conveyed

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.893
that gives you pause.

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:01.920
You know, and then I go back to my team

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.900
and I'm like, "Hey, hun,
what do you think of this?"

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:07.462
And they're like, "Wow, you can give any"

00:16:07.462 --> 00:16:10.550
And you're like, well, what
are we allowed to discuss?

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:13.460
You know, what is the conspiracy?

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:15.570
You know, conspiracy theory
is something that any

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.920
two or three people come up with.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.620
You know, when is it

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:23.860
bad and when is it,
you know, like prudent?

00:16:23.860 --> 00:16:24.693
I don't know.

00:16:24.693 --> 00:16:25.989
I don't know.

00:16:25.989 --> 00:16:26.930
- And it's hard to find the truth

00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:28.980
beneath everything too with all the,

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.440
I mean, so how quickly someone can just

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.100
say something that goes viral
that somehow becomes truthful

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:36.550
even though it's completely nonfactual.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:38.520
It doesn't navigate through that whole...

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:42.760
- And the mental strain

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.770
that it puts on people of
goodwill trying to navigate

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.143
those decisions and
those emotions and those,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.630
you know, particularly
when you have people

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:55.480
that you're responsible for.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:57.640
Now, when it comes to

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:02.140
rebuilding a society.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.140
Mental society,

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.823
where we can, you know,

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:09.350
relate with each other just takes

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:12.917
commitment and focus.

00:17:12.917 --> 00:17:15.310
And I think, and it's exhausting.

00:17:15.310 --> 00:17:16.221
- Yeah.

00:17:16.221 --> 00:17:17.160
And I know you've talked
about that before too,

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.720
how you see mental health
as the biggest challenge

00:17:19.720 --> 00:17:21.470
that we have as a nation,

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:23.680
especially coming out of the pandemic.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.980
So just thinking about, you know,

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:27.820
some of the things you've
done in the community like

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:29.920
what's been your idea of ways to

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:31.437
kind of help people move
through that because it is,

00:17:31.437 --> 00:17:33.900
you know, a lot of people
kind of talked about

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:36.010
the second wave being kind
of the mental health crisis.

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.090
Not that the crisis wasn't there before,

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:41.360
but just the additional
strain of all the isolation.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.210
And there's the fear factor people...

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:48.860
- Maybe the COVID has made it easier

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:52.010
for people to have a mental
health discussion because

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:54.260
on some macro level I hope

00:17:56.670 --> 00:18:01.670
because nobody did anything
quote, unquote, wrong.

00:18:01.910 --> 00:18:03.903
A lot of times in the stigma game,

00:18:04.850 --> 00:18:09.800
the idea of starting to talk
about one's mental health

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:15.190
connotes failure or, you know,

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.303
that it means that you've,

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:23.920
you know, that there's
been some bad behavior,

00:18:23.920 --> 00:18:26.950
bad thinking, bad choices, bad,

00:18:26.950 --> 00:18:28.610
I don't know what, but that

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.980
in some way you are responsible,

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.570
not just for like, you
know, the decisions you make

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:35.400
and the ramifications
of your decisions but

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.560
you're responsible for the very nature of

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:38.790
whether or not you're healthy.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:41.943
Your food choices, your
exercise, your sleep regimen,

00:18:41.943 --> 00:18:45.420
all those kinds of things
that go into health.

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:46.803
But there's, you know,

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:49.816
that's that stigma.

00:18:49.816 --> 00:18:53.070
And now everybody is
allowed to talk about,

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:56.820
wow, what does it mean that
you did night, you know,

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:01.820
18 months differently
than any one in society

00:19:01.900 --> 00:19:03.270
for the last couple of hundred years,

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:05.340
even through world wars
nothing happened like this.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.700
I asked my father, if you
remember the depression, he's 91.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:09.870
And he said, he remembered it very well.

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:10.960
He remembered bread lines.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:11.930
Since dad was the scientist,

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.067
but he remembered certain things.

00:19:13.067 --> 00:19:15.060
And I said," What's worse, that or this?"

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:16.360
He said this by a mile.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:19.450
He goes, you,

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:22.100
when someone was in trouble
during the depression,

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:23.730
you could help them.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:25.510
Now, the best thing you
can do to help somebody

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:27.500
was stay away from them.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:31.790
So that paradigm is in inhuman.

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:33.240
It's not human.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.810
When else in human history,

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:37.910
I mean, there have been
other plagues and other,

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.870
you know, mass health events and stuff,

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:43.853
but on this scale, you know.

00:19:45.329 --> 00:19:49.460
So, but any anyhow what
do I do in a weird way,

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:50.933
You know, "Lord Of The Rings"

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:54.120
maybe the biggest thing I ever do.

00:19:54.120 --> 00:19:59.120
I do a lot of other things like
specific to giving talks and

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:02.390
my organization and stuff like that but,

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:07.250
people, you know, there's a
great speech in the two towers.

00:20:07.250 --> 00:20:08.947
And Sam's looking out and he goes,

00:20:08.947 --> 00:20:11.390
"It's like in the great tales, Mr. Frodo.

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:13.580
So like in the great stories, Mr. Frodo,

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:15.750
Full of darkness and danger they were,

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:17.390
and sometimes you didn't
want to know the end

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:19.560
because how could the end be happy?

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:22.400
How could the world go on the way it was

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.703
when so much bad had happened?

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:30.910
But in the end, it's only a
passing thing that shadow.

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:33.930
Even darkness must pass
on a new day will come

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:36.480
and when the sun shines, it
will shine out the clearer.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.400
Those are the stories
that stayed with you.

00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.320
That meant something even
if you were too small

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.050
to understand why.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.030
I think Mr. Frodo, I do understand now.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:46.340
Folk in those stories had lots
of chances to turn them back.

00:20:46.340 --> 00:20:47.330
Only they didn't.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:49.447
They kept going because they
were holding onto something."

00:20:49.447 --> 00:20:51.037
"What are we holding onto Sam?"

00:20:51.037 --> 00:20:54.210
"That there's some good left
in this world, Mr. Frodo.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:55.910
and it's worth fighting for"

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:58.960
One of the greatest speeches
ever in the history of,

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:01.430
you know, drama or whatever.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:03.823
I'm so lucky I got to
be the one to say that.

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:07.690
And, you know, when you,

00:21:07.690 --> 00:21:09.880
when I'm giving talks
mental health speeches

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:12.443
to mental health organizations
which I do all the time,

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:17.120
something about relating my
direct personal experience

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:23.060
and reflecting the direct
personal experience of people

00:21:23.060 --> 00:21:24.990
in the audience.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:27.410
Often people will ask
questions and that's great

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:29.960
because then you can engage
a little bit like that.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.390
There's a sense of

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:38.960
security that people feel.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:40.950
There's a sense of relief that people feel

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:42.873
not to be alone in a moment.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:46.740
And then I'll do that speech
or then we'll play a little bit

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:47.880
of that speech or something like that.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.300
And you realize that like

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.890
they're, you know, in and of
itself that speech is powerful.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:55.810
I've had thousands.

00:21:55.810 --> 00:22:00.340
Literally thousands of people
over 20 years, tell me.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.260
Well, let's say hundreds
at the least say, you know,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:03.920
it saved their life
and it got them through

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:06.450
a dark time in their life that they,

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.160
you know, it gave them inspiration.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.210
It gave them hope to go on and it it'd be

00:22:11.316 --> 00:22:15.850
it gives, that speech gives
a prescription, you know.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:17.208
There is good.

00:22:17.208 --> 00:22:18.470
I don't know. Is there
good? Isn't there good?

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:21.610
Well, and that idea the
concept there is good

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.630
and it's worth fighting for.

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:25.400
My mental health

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:32.190
kind of exploration is
now is kind of graduated

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:35.840
into not that it's worth fighting for,

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.315
but like how do you fight?
- Yeah.

00:22:38.315 --> 00:22:39.900
(Sean chuckling)

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:41.420
- You know?

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:44.843
So which is, you know, I've got solutions,

00:22:45.819 --> 00:22:46.870
but they're just suggestions

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:49.220
that people have to find
them for themselves.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:50.853
With that movie and then,

00:22:52.360 --> 00:22:53.593
you know, just,

00:22:55.800 --> 00:22:58.720
I want my observations
about my mother's life

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.930
and her experience and
my life and my experience

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:01.853
with my family.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:04.920
And the fact that people
are willing to listen

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:06.710
a little bit closer
because they recognize you

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:08.020
from a movie or something.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.290
That was my mom's mission.

00:23:09.290 --> 00:23:11.960
Her mission was to
de-stigmatize mental health.

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:15.162
So I,

00:23:15.162 --> 00:23:19.460
I steer into that space where just

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:21.140
people like, well, I don't
know if you're comfortable.

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:22.430
Like, if I'm comfortable?

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:23.470
When I was like 12,

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:25.940
my mom was saying stuff
that you're like, "Mom,

00:23:25.940 --> 00:23:28.870
how did you say like,
you didn't get permission

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:31.180
before you told that anecdote, you know."

00:23:31.180 --> 00:23:34.110
Like, well, you know, and
I'm sorry on the one hand,

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:36.570
but then people come up to
me later in like saying,

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:38.430
you know, your mother's
book saved my life.

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.141
You know, your mother, her fight,

00:23:41.141 --> 00:23:42.760
her tenacity,

00:23:42.760 --> 00:23:45.010
her, you know,

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:48.020
her Moxie basically is
I gave them inspiration.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:49.480
And so we, you know...

00:23:49.480 --> 00:23:50.450
- There are time when
people weren't really

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:53.163
talking about it much, at
least in her profession.

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:56.190
- Well, the profession itself had advanced

00:23:56.190 --> 00:23:58.500
to a certain level at that point.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.040
Her diagnosis.

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:00.873
I mean, if you think,

00:24:00.873 --> 00:24:03.530
when she got diagnosed as a,

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:05.470
I think it was, was it
called manic depression then?

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:07.133
Now it's called bipolar disorder.

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:10.150
There was something else.

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.280
There was another one too.

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.080
She was afraid of like

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:20.990
padded rooms and straight
jackets, and electroshock therapy,

00:24:20.990 --> 00:24:24.430
and being ostracized, and
being given Thorazine.

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:26.710
And like, in her mind,

00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:29.410
when you're mentally unwell,

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:30.493
you go away.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:36.560
So the vocabulary, the science,
the psychopharmacology,

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:41.680
had all, you know, met her
where she was in her 40s.

00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:45.330
And then she decided to just
kind of like pick up this flag,

00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:48.390
like on the battlefield and
just start waving it around

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:52.053
because she was so grateful that,

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:56.400
she also saw it as a success
strategy for herself.

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:59.750
That if she took
ownership of her diagnosis

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:03.590
and her treatment and her, you know, her

00:25:04.730 --> 00:25:09.700
journey from places where
she behaved very badly

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:12.330
in a lot of ways in
front of a lot of people,

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:14.990
to someone who did that less and less.

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:17.330
And, you know,

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.540
if she took ownership of that journey

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:23.000
that she would sort of get credit for it.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:26.810
He would get credit for it,
from herself, from her family.

00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:28.450
We would probably the last
thing to give her credit for it

00:25:28.450 --> 00:25:31.630
because there was a disconnect between

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:33.890
the authority with which she spoke

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:35.960
and the truth of how she lived

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:38.400
and you're like there's a gap there.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:40.970
But it didn't matter to the people

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:42.150
who she was communicating with.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:43.733
They were inspired.

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:50.200
But that sort of empowerment
track that she was on,

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:51.033
I think

00:25:53.067 --> 00:25:54.363
was in terms of.

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:58.290
Larry King was always was, you know,

00:25:58.290 --> 00:26:02.110
the first cable news kind
of nighttime news anchor.

00:26:02.110 --> 00:26:03.810
I mean, you'd had the,

00:26:03.810 --> 00:26:08.070
you have Peter Jennings and
Dan Rather, and the other guy,

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:10.750
but who are your kind of like, you know,

00:26:10.750 --> 00:26:14.530
male, white, of a certain age, you know,

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:16.380
disseminating the news during the week

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:18.720
to let you know that
the world was okay and,

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:19.660
you know, whatever.

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:23.000
So, but Larry King was the
first like talk show guy to do

00:26:23.000 --> 00:26:25.920
those sorts of issues.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:27.500
War and stuff.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.360
And every time something would happen

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:30.930
in the mental health space,

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.023
Mariel Hemingway would be
on to deal with alcoholism,

00:26:38.616 --> 00:26:42.830
Rosalynn Carter would come
on to talk about depression,

00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:45.240
and my mom was there
to talk about bipolar.

00:26:45.240 --> 00:26:46.420
And there were, you know,

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:49.870
now we're all into seeing
panels of speakers on TV,

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:52.345
but at the time they were
doing it, that was pretty rare.

00:26:52.345 --> 00:26:54.890
So she was, you know, if somebody,

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:56.750
it was usually in response
to something horrible

00:26:56.750 --> 00:26:59.160
that would happen, a shooting
or something like that,

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:01.730
but she would get a third of the time

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:03.710
to respond to those issues.

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:06.514
And she educated herself
well enough, you know,

00:27:06.514 --> 00:27:08.650
laypersons education about it.

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:12.120
But, you know, sufficient to impress

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:15.870
Psychiatric Institute of
America who gave her a big award

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:20.550
and pharmaceutical companies
who had her, you know,

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:21.580
work with them and stuff.

00:27:21.580 --> 00:27:26.340
So I do have my, you mentioned
the Run3rd running thing.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:29.540
We ended up creating a nonprofit
called the Run3rd Alliance.

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:32.810
That's how you and I met because we,

00:27:32.810 --> 00:27:34.897
it had to go virtual. It's a 5K.

00:27:34.897 --> 00:27:38.130
It's a series of
afterschool running program

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.280
for elementary school kids.

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.410
So fifth, sixth graders.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:44.550
Fourth, fifth, sixth graders who

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:48.320
just a little bit of a very low cost

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:51.220
structured afterschool experience that,

00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:52.340
you know, running.

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:55.369
That's just so good for kids
to get that physical energy.

00:27:55.369 --> 00:27:58.130
and then to connect them

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.000
conceptually like with
their parents a little bit

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.550
and then with the community abroad.

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:05.220
And then it culminates in a 5K run at the,

00:28:05.220 --> 00:28:06.160
it's in Mesa, Arizona.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:08.670
We have like 10 schools, 12
schools and Mesa, Arizona.

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:12.590
And Red Mountain High
on their football field

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:15.740
we set up one of those 5K balloon things

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:16.587
and we bring a lot of people,

00:28:16.587 --> 00:28:19.290
you would get all kinds
of sponsors to come out,

00:28:19.290 --> 00:28:20.650
and it's a beautiful thing.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:23.830
So we had to go virtual because of the,

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:26.530
because of COVID and it occurred to us

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:28.220
as we were trying to figure out like,

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:30.740
you know, the fundraiser to be ready to.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:32.810
I mean, all the programs just folded

00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:35.240
when the close to shut up
was like, okay, well now.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.740
So do we want them to come back online?

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:39.830
We don't really have the
infrastructure set up for that.

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:41.520
We need money to be able to do it.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:45.170
So, but as we were putting
together the virtual 5K,

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:49.190
it just became clear
that one of the things

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:54.190
we could use the moment for
was to engage these families.

00:28:55.070 --> 00:28:59.023
Mostly lower income families
with mental health concepts.

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:04.160
And so, you know, when my mom died.

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:05.295
Right after she died,

00:29:05.295 --> 00:29:08.070
I cooked up this idea
of the mental health,

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:10.180
Patty Duke Mental Health Project.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.800
We never raised enough
money to actually be able to

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:16.680
launch it as a proper
instrument, legal instrument,

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:18.540
but we raised some money
and I was like, well,

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.100
let's put some money towards

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:23.770
facilitating a little dialogue with these,

00:29:23.770 --> 00:29:25.720
and it's not a dialogue, but like,

00:29:25.720 --> 00:29:27.883
how can we flow resources to these folks?

00:29:28.809 --> 00:29:29.800
And so we,

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:32.120
and I really appreciated the
fact that you can talk to us

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:33.390
about how to think about some of that.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.660
So those are the,

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:38.593
you know, mental health
speeches which I do a lot.

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:43.050
And those, and that little
thing that I just described,

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.480
or the kind of big
ticket things that I do.

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:46.444
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:29:46.444 --> 00:29:47.285
Well, it was definitely appreciated.

00:29:47.285 --> 00:29:48.690
I mean, I was thinking back
to something you said earlier

00:29:48.690 --> 00:29:50.280
about just a shift.

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:52.480
It's almost like people
have more permission

00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:53.313
to talk about it.

00:29:53.313 --> 00:29:54.960
Because, you know, you
talked about when people

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:55.970
are before it's,

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:58.000
a lot of times saying,
what did you do wrong?

00:29:58.000 --> 00:29:59.570
You know, this happened to you,

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:00.600
you're at fault.

00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:01.670
Have you noticed?

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:03.080
Is that openness still there

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.150
when you're engaging the community

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:07.290
with the Run-Through Alliance
when you're talking to,

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:08.430
are there still some pushback?

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:11.410
Like how do you navigate
that as someone who grew up

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:12.930
with someone who struggled
with mental health

00:30:12.930 --> 00:30:15.269
and saw kind of both sides of that?

00:30:15.269 --> 00:30:18.672
- I don't want to report
what I think I see.

00:30:18.672 --> 00:30:20.020
(all chuckling)

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:22.253
what I think I see are,

00:30:23.590 --> 00:30:25.190
I think there are,

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:27.160
the resources that are available to people

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:29.290
are so much more than people realize.

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:31.110
There are some great resources.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:34.350
So if people are in need,
everyone is in need.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:36.190
But if, to some extent or other,

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:38.280
but, you know, if it gets to
a point where people feel like

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:42.240
they need to, you know, talk
to a counselor or, you know,

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:44.330
you know, seek some sort of a diagnosis

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.230
or something like that,

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:50.620
if they ask a little
bit, there are routes.

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:52.240
You know, they'll get routed

00:30:52.240 --> 00:30:54.010
to places where they might be able to get

00:30:54.010 --> 00:30:56.200
something that they can afford.

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:57.680
You know, cause a lot of times there's

00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:59.600
people that are worried
about the expense of

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:00.980
that sort of thing, you know.

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:02.160
But I mean she's,

00:31:02.160 --> 00:31:03.280
I haven't really tried it,

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:05.050
but that Michael Phelps promoted thing

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:06.810
about talk therapy now kind of a thing,

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.810
you're like, well, that's, there you go.

00:31:09.758 --> 00:31:11.120
That's a lot of times
mostly would be, you know,

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:14.090
what people need at least
to start us function.

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:18.430
So, but I see antagonisms

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:22.050
developing along the lines of how people

00:31:22.050 --> 00:31:23.343
are coming out of COVID.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:25.863
- Wow.

00:31:25.863 --> 00:31:28.850
- And I see it

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:32.063
making people's communication
a little fragile.

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:39.630
So I think we could do well to start.

00:31:39.630 --> 00:31:42.330
You know, I had a friend
come to LA from New York

00:31:42.330 --> 00:31:43.163
and he was,

00:31:43.163 --> 00:31:46.180
he was clearly kind of back
in the saddle, you know.

00:31:46.180 --> 00:31:47.420
Some people are just, you know,

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:49.010
a lot of them, I mean, look,

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:50.960
stadiums of people are going to,

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:54.300
you know, like the country
is back for, you know,

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:56.003
in a serious way,

00:31:56.970 --> 00:31:59.300
but, you know, people's
mentality is still there.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:32:01.860
So he came out and I kept
saying, you know, listen Mike,

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:04.060
the way my wife and kids are,

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:05.725
they don't want me to, you know,

00:32:05.725 --> 00:32:07.730
go mix and mingle in the
world and then come back

00:32:07.730 --> 00:32:10.400
into their like protected bubble.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:13.640
And he, this my good friend
and he like couldn't,

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:15.010
he couldn't work with it for awhile

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:16.410
until I kind of said it forcefully.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:19.710
I'm like, look, it will be
upsetting to them if I do that,

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:21.460
if I come and meet you on somewhere

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:23.160
outside with Matt, whatever.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:26.387
And it's so and he finally was like,

00:32:26.387 --> 00:32:28.660
"Okay, I totally understand. That's fine."

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:29.610
But there was a,

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:34.010
there was we did have to
navigate that little space and

00:32:34.900 --> 00:32:39.200
I wasn't even the one
who was worried about it.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:40.790
So I then had to turn around,

00:32:40.790 --> 00:32:42.830
I had the static with him
and then I had to turn around

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:44.530
to my guys and be like, "Listen, you guys,

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:47.950
this is the environment that
people are operating in.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:51.690
So now behaving responsibly

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:54.770
at the extreme end that
you're wanting to is,

00:32:54.770 --> 00:32:56.940
you know, has consequences.

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.800
And we have to understand
what those consequences are."

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:01.450
And then they want to challenge them to.

00:33:01.450 --> 00:33:03.510
They want, you know, they want
friends, they want at school,

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:05.330
they want a lot of things but,

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.760
I just noticed that there's a look and

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:11.060
a kind of furtive look in people's eyes

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:13.003
when they ask questions about,

00:33:14.010 --> 00:33:15.830
you know, what's going on post COVID.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:20.283
And I it's makes me sad because I'm,

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.030
you know, I don't know if I'm an empath

00:33:23.030 --> 00:33:26.120
in the clinical sense
of it or the whatever

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:27.150
metaphysical sense of it,

00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:30.260
but I'm very in tune with what

00:33:30.260 --> 00:33:32.380
people are thinking and feeling.

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:33.703
And, you know,

00:33:34.914 --> 00:33:39.914
God, I remember when there was in 1992,

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:42.300
after the verdict within
the Rodney King trial

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:43.790
and the LA riots started.

00:33:43.790 --> 00:33:45.750
The 92 LA riots started.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:49.100
I remember driving to the grocery store

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:51.470
and we were like, okay, well, let's,

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:53.210
you know, let's get some

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:54.670
do a little grocery shopping

00:33:54.670 --> 00:33:56.320
because it's going to
be a few days probably

00:33:56.320 --> 00:33:58.110
until this all calms down.

00:33:58.110 --> 00:33:59.930
And I get to the grocery store

00:33:59.930 --> 00:34:03.430
and people in the parking lot

00:34:03.430 --> 00:34:08.070
were not observing normal rules.

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:09.350
They were not behaving normally.

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:12.033
They were parking a skew.

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:14.930
You know, they pull into the
parking lot and they'd stop

00:34:14.930 --> 00:34:15.763
and they'd get out.

00:34:15.763 --> 00:34:17.183
And, you know,

00:34:18.653 --> 00:34:20.190
you get 20 or 30 people who do that

00:34:20.190 --> 00:34:23.720
and the parking lot looks
like in Japan, you know.

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:25.840
So we go in and people are,

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:27.610
they're not in a,

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:29.550
they're not in a panic mode yet,

00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:32.100
but there's this simmering feeling.

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.370
And so we got and whatever
few little things.

00:34:34.370 --> 00:34:36.370
We weren't gonna like,
you know, order anything,

00:34:36.370 --> 00:34:37.660
but the few things we got,

00:34:37.660 --> 00:34:40.280
we get in line and we're standing there

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:44.810
and the person checking out
the items is not looking up.

00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:48.290
Or it's just only looking up
to the extent they need to.

00:34:48.290 --> 00:34:49.680
And I could see the person's name was,

00:34:49.680 --> 00:34:50.577
I don't know, Tom or something like that.

00:34:50.577 --> 00:34:52.440
I'm like, "Hey Tom, how you doing, man?

00:34:52.440 --> 00:34:54.230
How are things going?"

00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:55.320
And he looked up and he was,

00:34:55.320 --> 00:34:59.503
and you could feel
everyone around settled.

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:03.220
Somebody talked normal,

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:05.050
Somebody, you know, there was a,

00:35:05.050 --> 00:35:08.010
there was still a sense of like,

00:35:08.010 --> 00:35:09.750
we know things aren't right

